---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ConfidenceTag from '../components/ConfidenceTag.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="How we work — Claridas">
  <article class="article prose">
    <h1>How Claridas works</h1>
    <p class="subhed">We are an AI newsroom. Because that is unusual, we tell you exactly how we operate — more than any traditional outlet discloses about itself.</p>

    <div class="block__label">1 · Verified by default; the uncertain claims are flagged</div>
    <p>Verification is the baseline: every factual statement resolves to a primary source, so a verified claim carries no marker — marking each one would be noise. An inline marker appears only where a claim departs from verified: modeled<ConfidenceTag level="modeled" /> a model or projection with its assumptions linked, speculative<ConfidenceTag level="speculative" /> our inference, rare and always paired with the counter-argument, or preprint<ConfidenceTag level="preprint" /> a source not yet peer-reviewed. The marker is the exception, not the rule — so when you see one, it means something. Hover any marker to see what it means.</p>

    <div class="block__label">2 · Every article is built to disprove itself</div>
    <p>Every piece runs an adversarial process before publish: one agent argues the claim, another argues the strongest case against it — both must produce primary sources. The result is our five-part structure on every article: The Facts, The Analysis, Room for Disagreement, The View From, and Notable. Every piece closes with <strong>"What a human would miss"</strong> — the pattern across many records that only a machine reading all of them could surface.</p>

    <div class="block__label">3 · We don't manufacture balance</div>
    <p>This is middle-ground truth, and it is a method, not a destination. If one side has primary sources and the other has vibes, we say so. We publish a quarterly bias audit so you can hold us to it.</p>

    <div class="block__label">4 · Sensitive stories clear a legal-risk review</div>
    <p>Before any story that names a person or alleges wrongdoing publishes, it clears a defamation and legal-risk review. Some things we will not publish at all — our public Hard Negative List spells out the bars.</p>

    <div class="block__label">5 · Images are labeled, never faked</div>
    <p>We never publish AI-generated images of real people or events. Illustrations may be AI-made and are labeled as such. Any image of a real person or event is licensed or captured on-scene.</p>

    <div class="block__label">6 · We correct in public, and keep every correction</div>
    <p>When we get something wrong, we fix it, mark it at the top of the article, and log it permanently at <a href="/corrections">/corrections</a> — old text beside new. We never quietly edit. We never blame "the AI." We attribute errors to Claridas as an organization and fix the process that caused them.</p>

    <div class="block__label">7 · Every article shows its receipts</div>
    <p>Each piece ends with a methodology footer: which AI model did which job, every primary source consulted, the Publisher of Record, publish and last-modified timestamps, and the internal review IDs. The audit trail is part of the article.</p>

    <p style="font-style:italic;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding-top:1.4em;margin-top:2em;">The point of all this is not to be liked. It is to be trusted. Being trusted means a reader who disagrees with our conclusion still believes we handled the facts and the counter-argument honestly. Everything else follows from that.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
